CHAPTER I GENERAL PROVISIONS

Article 1 (Purpose) The purpose of this Decree is to stipulate matters mandated by the Forestry and Mountain Villages Development Promotion Act and matters necessary for its implementation.

Article 2 (Scope of Foresters) The term "person prescribed by Presidential Decree" in subparagraph 2 of Article 2 of the Forestry and Mountain Villages Development Promotion Act (hereinafter referred to as the "Act") refers to a person who falls under any of the following subparagraphs: <Amended on Jun. 4, 2009>
  1. A person who manages forestry in forest of 3 hectares or more;
  2. A person who engages in forestry for at least 90 days per year;
  3. A person whose annual sales of forest products through forestry management exceed 1.2 million won;
  4. A person who manages forestry as a member of the cooperatives according to Article 18 of the Forestry Cooperatives Act.

Article 3 (Requirements for Independent Foresters) In subparagraph 5 of Article 2 of the Act, "persons ..., meeting the requirements prescribed by Presidential Decree" refers to the following persons: <Amended on Jun. 4, 2009; Sep. 11, 2014; Dec. 24, 2018; Jul. 2, 2019>
  1. Individual forester:
    (a) Model forester: A person who is managing forests of 300 hectares or more (including income-sharing forests (distributed forests) and national forests leased for afforestation purposes, hereinafter the same in this Article} in an exemplary manner in accordance with a forest management plan, or a person who has afforestation performance of 100 hectares or more and is managing forests in an exemplary manner according to a forest management plan;
    (b) Excellent forester: A person who manages a forest of 100 hectares or more in an exemplary manner according to a forest management plan, or a person with afforestation performance of 50 hectares or more (20 hectares or more in the case of fruit-bearing trees) who manages a forest in an exemplary manner according to a forest management plan;
    (c) Self-employed forester: A person who manages a forest of 5 hectares or more in an exemplary manner according to a forest management plan, or a person who has afforested over 3 hectares of fruit-bearing trees and manages the forest in an exemplary manner in accordance with a forest management plan;
  2. Corporate forester: A corporation that meets any of the following criteria:
    (a) A corporation that manages forests of 300 hectares or more in an exemplary manner according to a forest management plan, or a corporation that has afforestation performance of 100 hectares or more and manages forests in an exemplary manner according to a forest management plan;
    (b) A corporation among agricultural corporations defined in subparagraph 2 of Article 2 the Act on Fostering and Supporting Agricultural and Fisheries Business Entities that manages forests of 10 hectares or more in an exemplary manner according to a forest management plan, or a corporation with afforestation performance of 5 hectares or more that manages forests in an exemplary manner according to a forest management plan.

Article 4 (Financial Support) The term "business prescribed by Presidential Decree" in subparagraph 7 of Article 4 of the Act refers to any of the following business projects:
  1. Project to improve management structure for cooperative management, agency management, and increased forestry income pursuant to Article 6 of the Act;
  2. Project to improve the distribution structure of forest products, including modernization of forest product distribution facilities;
  3. Projects to increase income through production, processing, distribution and export of forest products and research, development and fostering of forest product income sources;
  4. Projects of using preservative;
  5. Research, development and production of forest seeds (including grafted shoots, cuttings, and mushroom spawn; hereinafter the same shall apply) and forestry seedlings (including ornamental trees; hereinafter the same shall apply);
  6. Training of forestry technicians and development and distribution of forestry machinery and equipment;
  7. Expenses required for the operation and business of the Forestry Cooperatives and the National Forestry Cooperatives Federation;
  8. Development of natural recreation forests, arboretums, nature exhibition halls, and forest bathing areas;
  9. Training of independent foresters and forestry successors;
  10. Landscaping tree (including bonsai) cultivation business;
  11. Wildlife breeding business;
  12. Overseas forest resources survey and development projects;
  13. Advanced forestry technology development projects;
  14. Projects determined by the Administrator of the Korea Forest Service to promote forestry, such as subtropical forest restoration projects;
  15. Project to install heating facilities that utilize forest biomass (referring to woody forest products produced in forests; hereinafter the same shall apply) as energy, and project to collect, distribute, and process forest biomass.

CHAPTER 2 STRUCTURAL IMPROVEMENT OF FORESTRY

Article 5 (Collaborative Management) (1) Collaborative management according to Article 6 (1) of the Act refers to forestry management in which private forest owners jointly conduct afforestation projects, forest management projects, and production, sales, and processing of forest products in order to increase productivity and reduce management costs. <Amended on Jan. 25, 2012>
  (2) Deleted. <Jun. 4, 2009>
  (3) The Administrator of the Korea Forest Service and the heads of local governments may provide preferential support to those who engage in collaborative management in accordance with paragraph (1) by providing them with subsidies necessary for forestry projects. <Amended on Apr. 16, 2013>
  (4) If it is necessary to revitalize collaborative management according to paragraph (1), the Administrator of the Korea Forest Service shall establish a master plan for collaborative management including the development and dissemination of collaborative management methods, and the head of a local government shall formulate a collaborative management plan suitable for local conditions and notify the National Forestry Cooperatives Federation. <Amended on Apr. 16, 2013>
  (5) Matters necessary for implementing collaborative management according to paragraph (1) shall be prescribed by Ordinance of the Ministry of Agriculture, Food and Rural Affairs. <Amended on Feb. 29, 2008, Mar. 23, 2013>

Article 6 (Agency Management) (1) Proxy forestry management according to Article 6 (1) of the Act refers to forestry management conducted by a person falling under any of the following subparagraphs in accordance with a contract concluded with a private forest owner who has difficulty managing forestry on his/her own due to insufficient capital or technology: <Amended on Jun. 4, 2009; Jan. 10, 2017; Jun. 27, 2023>
  1. A forester;
  2. Forestry Cooperatives and National Forestry Cooperatives Federation;
  3. The head of a Si (including an administrative city mayor under the Special Act on the Establishment of Jeju Special Self-Governing Province and the Development of Free International City; hereinafter the same shall apply) /Gun/Gu (refers to the head of an autonomous Gu; hereinafter the same shall apply);
  4. Head of the Regional Forest Service or the head of a national forest management office of the Regional Forest Service;
  5. Universities with forestry departments (including technical colleges);
  5-2. A forestry business corporation registered as a corporation that can conduct all forestry projects according to subparagraph 1 , 2, and 4, of attached Table 2 of the Enforcement Decree of the Creation and Management of Forest Resources Act;
  6. Other corporations whose main business is forest management.
  (2) The Administrator of the Korea Forest Service and the heads of local governments may provide necessary support for proxy management to those conducting proxy management pursuant to paragraph (1). <Newly Inserted on Apr. 16, 2013>
  (3) The head of a Si/Gu/Gu who has jurisdiction over the location of the private forest managed by the proxy manager who received support in accordance with paragraph (2) shall report the status of proxy management for the relevant year to the Administrator of the Korea Forest Service through the Mayor/Do Governor by the end of February of the following year. <Newly Inserted on Apr. 16, 2013>
  (4) Matters necessary for implementing proxy forestry management according to paragraph (1) shall be prescribed by Ordinance of the Ministry of Agriculture, Food and Rural Affairs Ordinance. <Amended on Feb. 29, 2008; Mar. 23, 2013; Apr. 16, 2013>

Article 7 (Part-Time Forestry and Others) (1) The part-time, full-time, or corporate forestry according to Article 6 (2) of the Act refer to the following forestry:
  1. Part-time forestry: Forestry that manages forestry in a forest of 3 hectares or more or that is capable of production activities using forest products as the main raw material for at least 90 days a year;
  2. Full-time forestry: Forestry that manages forestry in a forest of 50 hectares or more or that can conduct production activities using forest products as the main raw material for at least 200 days a year;
  3. Corporate forestry: A corporation that manages forestry in a forest of 500 hectares or more, and can carry out production activities using forest products as the main raw material for at least 200 days a year.
  (2) When the Administrator of the Korea Forest Service or the head of a local government formulate policies on part-time forestry, full-time forestry, and corporate forestry in accordance with Article 6 (2) of the Act, the following matters shall be included:
  1. Matters concerning the development and dissemination of management technologies to promote part-time forestry, full-time forestry, and corporate forestry;
  2. Matters concerning the training and support of part-time foresters, full-time foresters, and corporate foresters.
  (3) Matters necessary for the implementation of part-time forestry, full-time forestry, and corporate forestry under paragraph (1) shall be prescribed by Ordinance of the Ministry of Agriculture, Food and Rural Affairs. <Amended on Feb. 29, 2008; Mar. 23, 2013>

Article 8 (Development and Fostering of Forest Products as Income Sources) (1) The items eligible for support as income sources of forest products according to Article 8 (2) of the Act shall be trees, mushrooms, wild greens, medicinal herbs, medicinal products, tree by-products, ornamental forest plants, and other forest products prescribed by the Ordinance of the Ministry of Agriculture, Food and Rural Affairs. <Amended on Feb. 29, 2008; Mar. 23, 2013; Sep. 11, 2014; May 28, 2018>
  (2) The head of a Si/Gun/Gu who wishes to be designated as a zone for the development of income sources concerning forest products (hereinafter referred to as "main production complex") pursuant to Article 8 (2) of the Act shall apply to the Administrator of the Korea Forest Service, the Special Metropolitan City Mayor, a Metropolitan City Mayor, a Do Governor, a Special Self-Governing Province Governor, or a Special Self-Governing City Mayor (hereinafter referred to as "Mayor/Do Governor"), as prescribed by Ordinance of the Ministry of Agriculture, Food and Rural Affairs. <Amended on Feb. 29, 2008; Jun. 4, 2009; Jan. 25, 2012; Mar. 23, 2013>
  (3) In the cases where the Administrator of the Korea Forest Service or the Mayor/Do Governor receives an application for designation of a main production complex from the head of a Si/Gun/Gu in accordance with paragraph (2), and if it is deemed necessary to designate a main production complex, the Administrator of the Korea Forest Service or the Mayor/Do Governor may designate a main production complex: provided, if it is deemed necessary to control the production and shipment of forest products, the Administrator of the Korea Forest Service or the Mayor/Do Governor may designate a main production complex at his/her discretion. <Amended on Jan. 25, 2012>
  (4) If the head of a Si/Gun/Gu deems it necessary to change or revoke the designation of an area as a major production complex, he or she may apply for the change or revocation of the designation to the Administrator of the Korea Forest Service or the Mayor/Do Governor, stating the grounds therefor. <Amended on Jan. 25, 2012>
  (5) The Administrator of the Korea Forest Service or the Mayor/Do Governor may change or revoke the designation of an area as a main production complex if there is an application pursuant to paragraph (4) or if it is deemed necessary to control the production or shipment of forest products. <Amended on Jan. 25, 2012>
  (6) When the Administrator of the Korea Forest Service or the Mayor/Do Governor designates, changes, or revokes a major production complex pursuant to paragraph (3) or (5), he or she shall publicly announce it. <Amended on Jan. 25, 2012>
  (7) The Administrator of the Korea Forest Service or the Mayor/Do Governor may request the head of a Si/Gun/Gu to submit data on the operational performance and evaluation of the main production complex designated pursuant to paragraph (3), as prescribed by the Ordinance of the Ministry of Agriculture, Food and Rural Affairs. In this case, the head of a Si/Gun/Gu shall submit the data to the Administrator of the Korea Forest Service or the Mayor/Do Governor. <Amended on Feb. 29, 2008; Jan. 25, 2012; Mar. 23, 2013>
  [Title Amended on Sep. 11, 2014]

Article 8-2 Deleted. <Jan. 10, 2017>

Article 9 (Scope of Public Institutions) In Article 11 (2) of the Act, "public institutions" refers to the following institutions:
  1. Government agencies and local governments;
  2. A corporation in which the State or a local government has contributed paid-in capital;
  3. Institutions funded by the State or local governments.

Article 10 Deleted. <May 22, 2013>

Article 10-2 Deleted. <May 22, 2013>

Article 11 (Modification and Termination of Contract for Reserve of Standing Timber) (1) Pursuant to Article 13 (3) of the Act, the contract for reserves of standing timber pursuant to paragraph (1) of the same Article (hereinafter referred to as "the contract for reserves of standing timber") may be changed in any of the following cases:
  1. In case the forest owner has repaid in advance part of the funds subsidized in accordance with Article 13 (4) of the Act;
  2. In cases the accumulation of standing timber has decreased compared to the time of concluding the contract for reserves of standing timber because of logging or other reasons specified in subparagraphs 1 and 2 of Article 15 of the Act.
  (2) A contract for the reserves of standing timber pursuant to Article 13 (3) of the Act may be terminated in any of the following cases:
  1. In cases where the contract for reserves of standing timber is concluded through fraud or other improper means;
  2. If the forest owner violates the terms and conditions of the standing timber reserve contract;
  3. In cases where all of the standing timber for which the contract for the reserve of standing timber has been concluded are lost due to forest fire damage, etc.;
  4. In cases where the forest owner has repaid in advance all of the funds received in accordance with Article 13 (4) of the Act.

Article 12 (Restrictions on Actions) In subparagraph 3 of Article 15 of the Act, "other cases prescribed by Presidential Decree" refers to any of the following cases: <Amended on Feb. 29, 2008; Jan. 25, 2012; Mar. 23, 2013>
  1. In the cases of digging or collecting plants such as aquatic plants, grass, flowers, moss, herbs, vines, or mushrooms, within the range that does not hinder the growth of trees;
  2. In the cases of carrying out forest tending work necessary for improving the form and quality of standing timber and promoting the growth thereof with the consent of persons prescribed by Ordinance of the Ministry of Agriculture, Food and Rural Affairs.

Article 13 (Use and Support of Forests) The "projects prescribed by Presidential Decree" in Article 16 (1) 4 of the Act refers to any of the following projects:
  1. Projects for creating a nature observation center;
  2. Bonsai production project.

Article 14 (Selection of Independent Foresters) (1) A person who wishes to be selected as an independent forester in accordance with Article 17 (2) of the Act shall submit documents proving that he/she meets the requirements for independent forester in accordance with Article 3, and apply to the Administrator of the Korea Forest Service or the Mayor/Do Governor through the head of a Si/Gun/Gu. <Amended on Jan. 25, 2012>
  (2) Upon receipt of an application filed under paragraph (1), the Administrator of the Korea Forest Service or the Mayor/Do Governor shall verify whether the application satisfies the requirements specified in Article 3, and select a person who satisfies the requirements as an independent forester, and issue a certificate of selection as an independent forester. <Amended on Jan. 25, 2012>
  (3) The details necessary for the selection procedure and aftercare of independent foresters shall be prescribed by the Administrator of the Korea Forest Service.

Article 15 (Support for Independent Farmers) (1) The Administrator of the Korea Forest Service or the Mayor/Do Governor may provide support to an independent forester with priority over others in regard to the following matters: <Amended on Jan. 25, 2012>
  1. Subsidy or loan of funds necessary for forestry management;
  2. Support for equipment required for forestry management;
  3. Awards and overseas training for independent foresters with excellent management performance;
  4. Support for technical training and information provision necessary for forestry management.
  (2) If necessary for fostering independent foresters, the Administrator of the Korea Forest Service or the Mayor/Do Governor may provide support pursuant to paragraph (1) to a corporation comprised of independent foresters and established under Article 32 of the Civil Act. <Amended on Jan. 25, 2012>

Article 15-2 (Support for Education in Forestry Sector) (1) Pursuant to Article 17-2 (1) of the Act, the Administrator of the Korea Forest Service may provide educational support in the forestry sector to affiliated schools in the forestry and forest-related fields that meet all of the following requirements:
  1. It shall have an operational plan for educational curriculum that is appropriate for the supply and demand of manpower in the forestry sector and for the development of professional competencies;
  2. It shall have the personnel and facilities to carry out human resources training projects in the forestry field;
  3. It shall meet other requirements, such as plans to strengthen educational capacity in the forestry sector, as determined and publicly notified by the Administrator of the Korea Forest Service.
  (2) In the case of providing educational support in the forestry sector pursuant to paragraph (1), the Minister of the Korea Forest Service may cover necessary expenses, such as the development of educational programs and the purchase of practice equipment, within budgetary limits.
  (3) Except as otherwise provided for in paragraphs (1) and (2), the Administrator of the Korea Forest Service shall determine and publicly notify the necessary details regarding applications for educational support, methods and procedures for educational support in the forestry field, etc.
  [This Article Newly Inserted on Sep. 19, 2017]

Article 15-3 (Projects for Employment Creation and Business Startup Promotion in the Forestry Sector) Pursuant to Article 17-2 (2) of the Act, the Administrator of the Korea Forest Service may conduct the following projects to create jobs and promote business start-up in the forestry sector:
  1. Project on fact-finding survey on the current status of employment and start-ups in the forestry sector, public awareness of the forestry sector, etc.;
  2. Project on establishing and operating an employment information network in the forestry sector;
  3. Projects related to improving public awareness and promotion in the forestry sector;
  4. Projects concerning the discovery, fostering, support, etc. of entrepreneurs in the field of forestry;
  5. Projects concerning education and training for the creation of employment in the field of forestry and the revitalization of start-ups;
  6. Other projects deemed necessary by the Administrator of the Korea Forest Service to create jobs and promote start-ups in the forestry sector.
  [This Article Newly Inserted on Sep. 19, 2017]

Article 16 (Training of Forestry Technicians) (1) The Administrator of the Korea Forest Service and the Mayor/Do Governor may implement the following projects to foster technicians in the forestry field in accordance with Article 18 (1) of the Act: <Amended on Jan. 25, 2012>
  1. Job training for forestry technicians;
  2. Job placement and employment stability for forestry technicians;
  3. Support of forestry machinery and equipment to forest management association pursuant to paragraph (2);
  4. Improvement of working conditions, including safety management in the workplace and welfare of forestry workers.
  (2) The Administrator of the Korea Forest Service and the Mayor/Do Governor may, in order to provide job placement and ensure employment stability pursuant to paragraph (1) 2, require technicians in the forestry field to organize a forest management association to perform the following projects as contracted projects: <Amended on Jan. 25, 2012; Jul. 9, 2019>
  1. Forestation projects;
  2. Forest-tending projects;
  3. Projects to prevent forest diseases and pests;
  4. Nursery business;
  5. Projects to cut, uproot, or transplant standing trees;
  6. Other projects that the Administrator of the Korea Forest Service deems necessary and publicly announces for job placement and employment stability for technicians in the forestry field.
  (3) The number of members of the forest management association under paragraph (2) shall be 6 or more but 30 or less, and the types of forest management associations and the required personnel standards shall be as follows: <Amended on Feb. 29, 2008; Jan. 25, 2012; Mar. 23, 2013; Sep. 11, 2014; Nov. 27, 2018>
  1. Forest management associations of technicians:
  The number of forest management engineers of level 2 or higher as set forth in attached Table 3 of the Enforcement Decree of the Act on Promotion and Management of Forestry Technology shall be equal to or greater than the ratio specified in the following:
    (a) If the number of association members is 6 or more but less than 10: 60 percent;
    (b) If the number of members is 11 or more but less than 30: 50 percent;
  2. Mechanized forest management association:
  An organization that meets the requirements of forest management associations of technicians referred to in subparagraph 1, and at least 30 percent of the total members of which shall consist of persons who have completed technical training in forestry machinery and equipment at a forestry training institution designated by the Ordinance of the Ministry of Agriculture, Food and Rural Affairs;
  (4) Matters necessary for the period of education pursuant to paragraph (3) 1 and 2 shall be prescribed by Ordinance of the Ministry of Agriculture, Food and Rural Affairs. <Amended on Feb. 29, 2008; Mar. 23, 2013>
  (5) The Administrator of the Korea Forest Service and the Mayor/Do Governor may provide forestry machinery and equipment if necessary to revitalize the forest management associations. <Amended on Jan. 25, 2012>
  (6) Matters necessary for the support and management of forestry technicians shall be prescribed by Ordinance of the Ministry of Agriculture, Food and Rural Affairs. <Amended on Feb. 29, 2008; Mar. 23, 2013>

Article 17 Deleted. <Jan. 25, 2012>

CHAPTER III MANAGEMENT OF SPECIALLY MANAGED FOREST PRODUCTS

Article 17-2 (Specialized Institutions) (1) The institutions or organizations specialized in quality control of forest products that are established pursuant to the main clause of Article 18-2 (1) of the Act (hereinafter referred to as "specialized institutions") are as follows: <Amended on Jan. 25, 2012>
  1. Korean Forestry Promotion Institute pursuant to Article 29-2 of the Act;
  2. An institution or organization possessing the personnel, equipment, and facilities necessary for the performance of forest product quality control tasks, and designated and publicly notified as a specialized institution by the Administrator of the Korea Forest Service.
  (2) The details necessary for the standards for personnel, equipment, facilities, etc. under paragraph (1) 2, and the procedures and methods for designation and public notification thereof shall be prescribed by the Administrator of the Korea Forest Service. <Amended on Sep. 19, 2017>
  [This Article Newly Inserted on Jul. 14, 2011]

Article 17-3 (Production Suitability Survey) (1) "Cases prescribed by Presidential Decree" in the proviso of Article 18-2 (1) of the Act refers to any of the following cases:
  1. In case of production for experimental or educational purposes;
  2. In case of experimental or educational purposes;
  3. In other cases, where the Administrator of the Korea Forest Service determines and publicly notify in consideration of the production purpose, production period, and production quantity of specially managed forest products.
  (2) The "standards prescribed by Presidential Decree" in Article 18-2 (2) of the Act refers to the standards set forth in attached Table 2.
  [This Article Newly Inserted on Jul. 14, 2011]

Article 17-4 (Confirmation of Production Process) (1) A person who produces specially managed forest products in accordance with Article 18-3 (1) of the Act (hereinafter referred to as "producer") shall record and manage the production process of specially managed forest products in accordance with the following standards:
  1. It shall be classified by working date;
  2. It shall be classify by seedlings and seeds, respectively;
  3. It shall include detailed descriptions of the tasks;
  4. It shall include information on the use of pesticides or fertilizers;
  5. It shall be recorded and managed in accordance with the methods deemed necessary by the Administrator of the Korea Forest Service, taking into consideration the characteristics of production process, the production area and quantity, etc.
  (2) The Administrator of the Korea Forest Service shall determine and publicly notify the necessary details regarding the procedures and formats for recording and management of the production process according to paragraph (1).
  (3) In Article 18-3 (2) of the Act, "pesticides or fertilizers prescribed by Presidential Decree" refers to the following pesticides or fertilizers: <Amended on Mar. 23, 2013; Mar. 8, 2022>
  1. Pesticides according to the Pesticide Control Act;
  2. Ordinary fertilizer according to the Fertilizer Control Act;
  3. By-product fertilizers according to the Fertilizer Control Act: provided, organic fertilizers and microbial fertilizers prescribed by Ordinance of the Ministry of Agriculture, Food and Rural Affairs shall be excluded.
  (4) The "period prescribed by Presidential Decree" in Article 18-3 (3) of the Act refers to the period within 5 months before or after the third anniversary of the date on which the production of specially managed forest products is reported pursuant to Article 18-2 (1) of the Act. <Amended on Sep. 19, 2017>
  [This Article Newly Inserted on Jul. 14, 2011]

Article 17-5 (Quality Inspection) (1) A producer who intends to undergo a quality inspection pursuant to the former part of Article 18-4 (1) of the Act or a person who imported specially managed forest products (hereinafter referred to as "importer") pursuant to the former part of Article 18-4 (1) of the Act shall apply for a quality inspection to a specialized agency as prescribed by Ordinance of the Ministry of Agriculture, Food and Rural Affairs by no later than 10 days prior to the date of inspection. <Amended on Mar. 23, 2013; Sep. 19, 2017>
  (2) When a specialized agency receives a request for quality inspection pursuant to paragraph (1), it shall conduct the quality inspection within 30 days from the date of receipt of the request, in accordance with the residual pesticide limits prescribed and publicly notified by the Minister of Food and Drug Safety pursuant to Article 7 of the Food Sanitation Act, and notify the applicant of the results: provided, in the case of unavoidable circumstances, the agency may inform the applicant of the circumstances and extend the period by up to 20 days. <Amended on Mar. 23, 2013; Sep. 19, 2017>
  (3) In case of passing the quality inspection according to paragraph (2), the validity period of the inspection shall be as follows. In this case, the validity period is calculated from the date of completion of the quality inspection:
  1. Quality inspection received by the producer: 2 years;
  2. Quality inspection received by importer: 1 year.
  (4) Except as otherwise provided in paragraphs (1) through (3), detailed matters necessary for the procedures and notification of quality inspection shall be prescribed and publicly notified by the Administrator of the Korea Forest Service.
  [This Article Newly Inserted on Jul. 14, 2011]

Article 17-6 (Objection to Quality Inspection Results) (1) If a person who has undergone a quality inspection pursuant to Article 18-4 (2) of the Act wishes to file an objection against the results of quality inspection, he or she shall submit a written objection to a specialized agency within 30 days from the date of notification of the results.
  (2) When a specialized agency receives an objection pursuant to paragraph (1), it shall review the validity of the application within 10 days from the date of receipt of the application, and notify the applicant of whether or not it has been accepted.
  (3) In cases where a specialized agency accepts an objection in accordance with paragraph (2), the quality inspection may be conducted on the date the producer or importer wishes to undergo the quality inspection within the period specified in each of the following subparagraphs:
  1. For producers: Within 1 year from the date of receipt of the objection;
  2. For importers: Within 30 days from the date of receipt of the objection.
  (4) Article 17-5 (2) through (4) shall apply to the quality inspection pursuant to paragraph (3).
  [This Article Newly Inserted on Jul. 14, 2011]

Article 17-7 (Methods of Disposal of Specially Managed Forest Products) The "method prescribed by Presidential Decree" in Article 18-5 (3) of the Act refers to any of the following methods:
  1. Incineration;
  2. Shredding;
  3. Other methods determined and publicly notified by the Administrator of the Korea Forest Service, taking into consideration the characteristics, quantity, and size of specially managed forest products.
  [This Article Newly Inserted on Jul. 14, 2011]

Article 17-8 (Quality Labeling) (1) In cases where a producer, importer, or seller intends to distribute, clear customs, or sell specially managed forest products pursuant to Article 18-6 (1) and (2) of the Act, the products must be packaged in a box or container of a size that can hold a quality inspection certificate (hereinafter referred to as "certificate") as prescribed by the Ordinance of the Ministry of Agriculture, Food and Rural Affairs, and the certificate must be attached. <Amended on Dec. 24, 2018>
  (2) The certificate shall include the following information: <Amended on Dec. 24, 2018>
  1. Name of producer or importer (in case of a corporation, the name of the corporation);
  2. Place of production or country of import;
  3. Quality inspection agency or organization, quality inspection number and quality inspection date;
  4. Validity period of quality inspection;
  5. Other matters that the Administrator of the Korea Forest Service deems necessary and publicly notify in relation to the results of quality inspection.
  (3) Deleted. <Dec. 24, 2018>
  [This Article Newly Inserted on Jul. 14, 2011]

CHAPTER III-II QUALITY CERTIFICATION OF FORESTRY MACHINERY AND EQUIPMENT

Article 17-9 (Quality Certification Standards for Forestry Machinery and Equipment) (1) The Administrator of the Korea Forest Service may provide quality certification in accordance with Article 18-12 (1) of the Act (hereinafter referred to as "quality certification") on forestry machinery and equipment that satisfy all of the following standards:
  1. To ensure the safety required for the use of forestry machinery and equipment;
  2. To have the performance required for the purpose of forestry machinery and equipment.
  (2) The Administrator of the Korea Forest Service shall prescribe and publicly notify the necessary details regarding the standards for quality certification specified in paragraph (1).
  [This Article Newly Inserted on Sep. 19, 2017]

Article 17-10 (Quality Certification Procedures and Methods) (1) A person who wishes to apply for quality certification in accordance with Article 18-12 (2) of the Act shall submit a quality certification application form as prescribed by the Ordinance of the Ministry of Agriculture, Food and Rural Affairs, along with the forestry machinery and equipment subject to quality certification to the Administrator of the Korea Forest Service: provided, in cases where it is difficult to store the forestry machinery and equipment subject to quality certification before the start of the quality certification examination or where the quality certification examination needs be conducted on-site, the forestry machinery and equipment subject to quality certification may be submitted at the time of the quality certification examination.
  (2) In the event that the Administrator of the Korea Forest Service receives an application for quality certification under paragraph (1), he/she shall examine whether it complies with the quality certification standards pursuant to Article 17-9, and issue a quality certificate for forestry machinery and equipment that is recognized to be in compliance with the standards as a result of the examination.
  (3) If the Administrator of the Korea Forest Service finds that the applicant fails to meet the quality certification standards as a result of the examination under paragraph (2), he or she shall notify the applicant of such fact without delay, specifying the grounds therefor.
  (4) In any of the following cases, the Administrator of the Korea Forest Service may reject a written application for quality certification submitted by an applicant pursuant to paragraph (1) or suspend the examination, and notify the applicant that the application is deemed unsuitable for quality certification. In such cases, the Administrator of the Korea Forest Service shall notify the applicant of the fact and grounds therefor in writing:
  1. In cases where it is significantly inappropriate to conduct quality certification on the relevant forestry machinery and equipment for the purpose of quality certification submitted by the applicant;
  2. In cases where the applicant fails to pay fees pursuant to subparagraph 3 of Article 30-2 of the Act;
  3. In cases where the applicant fails to submit forestry machinery and equipment subject to the examination of quality certification in accordance with paragraph (1);
  4. In cases where the examination cannot proceed due to damage or abnormality of forestry machinery equipment during the normal inspection process of quality certification;
  5. In cases where the applicant withdraws the application for quality certification.
  (5) Except as otherwise provided for in paragraphs (1) through (4), detailed matters necessary for the procedures and methods of quality certification shall be prescribed and publicly notified by the Administrator of the Korea Forest Service.
  [This Article Newly Inserted on Sep. 19, 2017]

Article 17-11 (Validity Period of Quality Certification) The validity period of quality certification shall be 3 years from the date of issuance of the quality certification pursuant to Article 17-10 (2).
  [This Article Newly Inserted on Sep. 19, 2017]

Article 17-12 (Objection to Examination Results of Quality Certification) (1) Any person who wishes to file an objection against the results of quality certification examination shall submit a written objection prescribed by Ordinance of the Ministry of Agriculture, Food and Rural Affairs to the Administrator of the Korea Forest Service within 30 days from the date of notification of the results.
  (2) When the Administrator of the Korea Forest Service receives an objection filed under paragraph (1), he/she shall review the validity of the objection within 10 days from the date of receipt of the written objection, and notify the applicant of the results of the review.
  [This Article Newly Inserted on Sep. 19, 2017]

CHAPTER IV DESIGNATION OF FORESTRY PROMOTION ZONES

Article 18 (Designation of Forestry Promotion Zones) (1) Forestry promotion zones under Article 19 (1) of the Act shall be designated as areas where the development and cultivation of income sources of forest products can revitalize forestry management, and shall be limited to forestry land pursuant to Article 4 (1) 1 (a) and Article 5 of the Mountainous Districts Management Act, yet such designation shall meet the following standards: <Amended on Jun. 4, 2009>
  1. An area where the ratio of forest land to the administrative districts of Si (including administrative Si prescribed in the Special Act on the Establishment of Jeju Special Self-Governing Province and the Development of Free International City; hereinafter the same shall apply)/ Gun, or autonomous Gu (hereinafter referred to as "Si/Gun/Gu") is 70 percent or more;
  2. An area where the ratio of forest land to administrative districts of Si/Gun/Gu is less than 70%, and which falls under any of the following items:
    (a) An area where a group of forests is collectively distributed, making it eligible for designation as a forestry promotion zone;
    (b) Areas where collaborative management is implemented in accordance with Article 5 (1), and where continuous intensive forestry management is deemed necessary;
    (c) Areas that are connected to the forestry promotion zones of adjacent Si/Gun/Gu, which are recognized as requiring forestry management.
  (2) The Administrator of the Korea Forest Service may request the Mayor/Do Governor to submit the following documents in order to designate a forestry promotion zone pursuant to Article 19 (1) 1 of the Act: <Amended on Jan. 25, 2012>
  1. Business plan for forestry promotion zone;
  2. Current status of parcel numbers and area by classification of forest use in the target area of the forestry promotion zone;
  3. Opinions of residents in the target area of the forestry promotion zone;
  4. Topographic map with a scale of 1:25,000 or more indicating the target area of the forestry promotion zone.
  (3) Matters necessary for the designation of forestry promotion zones pursuant to paragraph (1) shall be prescribed by Ordinance of the Ministry of Agriculture, Food and Rural Affairs. <Amended on Feb. 29, 2008, Mar. 23, 2013>

Article 19 (Change or Cancellation of Designation of Forestry Promotion Zone) Matters necessary for the procedures for the modification or revocation of designation of forestry promotion zones designated pursuant to Article 19 of the Act shall be prescribed by Ordinance of the Ministry of Agriculture, Food and Rural Affairs. <Amended on Mar. 23, 2013>
  [This Article Wholly Amended on Jun. 4, 2009]

Article 20 (Financial Support) When providing financial support for forestry projects, the Administrator of the Korea Forest Service may give priority support to forestry projects in forestry promotion zones over other forest areas.

Article 20-2 (Designation Standards for Special-Purpose Timber Production Areas) (1) In the case where the Administrator of the Korea Forest Service designates a special-purpose timber production area pursuant to Article 21-2 (1) of the Act, the following standards shall apply: <Amended on May 7, 2024>
  1. To be designated for national forests under the jurisdiction of the Korea Forest Service;
  2. To be designated by classifying for the purposes of plywood, national heritage restoration, and shiitake mushroom log use;
  3. To be designated in full consideration of the distribution and types of trees.
  (2) In cases where the Administrator of the Korea Forest Service deems it necessary to designate a special-purpose timber production area pursuant to paragraph (1), he or she may hear the opinions of timber-related institutions, organizations, or experts.
  (3) When the Administrator of the Korea Forest Service designates a special-purpose timber production area pursuant to paragraph (1), he or she shall publicly announce the fact on the website of the Korea Forest Service.
  (4) Except as otherwise provided for in paragraphs (1) through (3), matters necessary for the standards, procedures, and methods of designation of special-purpose timber production areas shall be prescribed and publicly notified by the Administrator of the Korea Forest Service.
  [Newly Inserted on Dec. 31, 2010]

Article 20-3 (Changes in Designation of Special-Purpose Timber Production Zones) (1) Article 20-2 (2) through (4) shall apply to the modification or revocation of designation of special-purpose timber production zones pursuant to Article 21-2 (3) of the Act.
  (2) "Facilities for official or public use prescribed by Presidential Decree" in Article 21-2 (3) 3 of the Act refers to the facilities that fall under any of the following subparagraphs: <Amended on Jul. 14, 2014>
  1. River facilities defined in subparagraph 3 of Article 2 of the River Act;
  2. Roads defined in subparagraph 1 of Article 2 of the Road Act;
  3. Railroad tracks referred to in subparagraph 2 (a) of Article 3 of the Framework Act on Railway Industry Development;
  4. Electric power source facilities defined in subparagraph 1 of Article 2 of the Electric Power Source Development Promotion Act;
  5. National communications facilities or broadcasting communications facilities defined in subparagraph 3 of Article 2 of the Framework Act on Broadcasting Communications Development;
  6. Observation facilities defined in Article 2 (2) 3 of the Weather Observation Standardization Act;
  7. Other facilities similar to those specified in subparagraph 1 through 6, which are designated by the Administrator of the Korea Forest Service.
  [Newly Inserted on Dec. 31, 2010]

CHAPTER V DEVELOPMENT OF MOUNTAIN VILLAGES

Article 21 (Formulation of Master Plans for Development of Mountain Villages) (1) Deleted. <Jan. 25, 2012>
  (2) When the Administrator of the Korea Forest Service establishes or changes the master plan for the development of mountain villages (hereinafter referred to as the "master plan") pursuant to Article 23 (1) of the Act and the annual implementation plans pursuant to Article 23 (4) of the Act, he/she shall announce the outline thereof without delay by publicly notifying it in the Official Gazette. <Amended on Jul. 2, 2019>
  (3) The Mayor/Do Governor (excluding a Special Self-Governing City Mayor; hereafter the same shall apply in this Article and Articles 22 and 25) shall formulate a mountain village development plan for the Special Metropolitan City, Metropolitan City, Do, or Special Self-Governing Province under Article 23 (7) of the Act (hereinafter referred to as "City/Do plan") within 1 year from the date of receipt of a master plan from the Administrator of the Korea Forest Service pursuant to Article 23 (5) of the Act. <Amended on Jun. 4, 2009; Jan. 25, 2012; Jul. 2, 2019; Jun. 2, 2020>
  (4) When the Mayor/Do Governor formulates a City/Do plan in accordance with paragraph (3), he/she shall notify the head of a Si/Gun/Gu of the plan without delay and publicly notify the outline.
  (5) The head of a Si/Gun/Gu shall formulate mountain villages development plan of Si/Gun/Gu (hereinafter referred to as "Si/Gun/Gu Plan") within 6 months from the date of receipt of notification of a City/Do Plan pursuant to paragraph (4) from the Mayor/Do Governor. <Amended on Jan. 25, 2012>
  (6) When the head of a Si/Gun/Gu formulates a Si/Gun/Gu plan in accordance with paragraph (5), he or she shall publicly notify its outline without delay.

Article 22 (Content and Methods of Survey on Mountain Villages) (1) The basic survey on mountain villages under Article 24 (1) of the Act and the detailed survey thereon under paragraph (2) of that Article shall include the following matters: <Amended on Jan. 25, 2012>
  1. Matters concerning the distribution and use of forest resources;
  2. Matters concerning population changes in mountain villages;
  3. Matters related to the mountain village economy, including mountain village income;
  4. Matters concerning the culture and traditions of mountain villages;
  5. Matters concerning human resources in mountain villages;
  6. Matters concerning exchanges between mountain villages and cities;
  7. Matters concerning green tourism and eco-tourism resources;
  8. Other matters necessary for the formulation of the master plans or City/Do Plans, or Si/Gun/Gu Plans.
  (2) The Administrator of the Korea Forest Service shall prepare guidelines for the basic survey or detailed survey pursuant to paragraph (1), and notify the Mayor/Do Governor or the head of a Si/Gun/Gu of the guidelines for the detailed survey. <Newly Inserted on Jan. 25, 2012>
  (3) The basic survey or detailed survey under paragraph (1) shall be conducted in accordance with the guidelines under paragraph (2), but in principle, the on-site survey shall be conducted, and the indirect survey method using hearings, statistical data, documents, etc. may be concurrently conducted. <Amended on Jan. 25, 2012>
  (4) When preparing the guidelines for survey, the Administrator of the Korea Forest Service shall consult with the National Institute of Forest Science and the academic community. <Amended on Jan. 25, 2012>
  (5) In Article 24 (4) of the Act, "an agency or organization prescribed by Presidential Decree" refers to any of the following institutions or organizations: <Newly Inserted on Jan. 25, 2012; Dec. 16, 2021>
  1. Korean Forestry Promotion Institute pursuant to Article 29-2 of the Act;
  2. A cooperative or central association pursuant to the Forestry Cooperatives Act;
  3. Schools defined in Article 2 of the Higher Education Act;
  4. Forestry-related testing and research institutes established by ordinance of local governments pursuant to Article 126 of the Local Autonomy Act.
  [Title Amended on Jan 25, 2012]

Article 23 (Persons Eligible to be Entrusted with the Implementation of Specialized Mountain Village Promotion Projects) In Article 26 of the Act, "persons prescribed by Presidential Decree" refers to the following persons: <Amended on Jun. 4, 2009; Dec. 31, 2010; Dec. 9, 2014; Jun. 2, 2020; Jun. 27, 2023>
  1. A cooperative or the National Forestry Cooperatives Federation under the Forestry Cooperatives Act;
  2. Korea Rural Community Corporation prescribed in the Korea Rural Community Corporation and Farmland Management Fund Act;
  3. Forestry business corporations that can carry out projects for the creation of natural recreation forest and the creation of mountain village eco-village according to subparagraph 5 of attached Table 2 of the Enforcement Decree of the Creation and Management of Forest Resources Act;
  4. Other persons recognized by the Administrator of the Korea Forest Service as suitable for implementing the specialized mountain village promotion projects.
  [Title Amended on Jun. 26, 2020]

Article 24 (Support for Forestry Projects) In Article 27 (3) of the Act, "Forestry projects prescribed by Presidential Decree" refers to the following projects: <Amended on Sep. 19, 2017; Jun. 2, 2020>
  1. Development and fostering of forest products as income sources pursuant to Article 8 of the Act;
  2. Projects for combined management of forests according to Article 9 of the Act;
  3. Forest product processing business according to Article 10 of the Act;
  4. Forest utilization business according to Article 16 of the Act;
  5. Training of forestry technicians pursuant to Article 18 of the Act;
  6. Projects for mechanization of forestry according to Article 18-11 of the Act.

Article 24-2 (Special Cases concerning Application of State Forest Administration and Management Act) The "separate standards prescribed by Presidential Decree" in Article 27-2 (2) of the Act refers to the standards in each of the following subparagraphs:
  1. It shall not be a national forest whose use is prohibited or restricted by the Act or other statutes or regulations or whose use plan is confirmed: provided, with respect to the national forests whose use plan is confirmed by the statutes and regulations under the jurisdiction of the Ministry of Agriculture, Food and Rural Affairs, a use permit or lease (hereinafter referred to as "lease, etc." in this Article) may be granted only if it does not interfere with the use purpose of the national forest according to the use plan;
  2. The usage of lease, etc. shall correspond to the purpose of conversion or temporary use of a mountainous district pursuant to Article 10 or 12 of Mountainous Districts Management Act, and it shall be a national forest that meets the standards for conversion or temporary use of a mountainous district pursuant to Articles 15, 15-2, or 18 of that Act;
  3. In cases where a disposition such as permission, authorization, approval, designation, registration, report or consultation is required in accordance with other statutes and regulations, such disposition shall be made.
  [This Article Newly Inserted on Jun. 2, 2020]

Article 24-3 (Designation of Mountainous Village Revitalization Support Center) (1) In Article 27-3 (1) 3 of the Act, "requirements prescribed by Presidential Decree, such as professional personnel and organizations" refers to the requirements in each of the following subparagraphs:
  1. To establish a dedicated organization consisting of at least 3 professional personnel with experience in forestry education, consultation on startups utilizing forest resources, and support services;
  2. The articles of incorporation or other business operation regulations shall specify as main projects the support projects for urban residents to settle in mountain villages and for revitalizing the local economy of mountain villages;
  3. To provide facilities and equipment necessary to support the revitalization of the local economy of mountain villages.
  (2) A person who wishes to be designated as a mountain village revitalization support center pursuant to Article 27-3 (1) of the Act shall submit to the Administrator of the Korea Forest Service an application for designation as prescribed by the Ordinance of the Ministry of Agriculture, Food and Rural Affairs, along with the following documents:
  1. Status of possession of professional manpower and dedicated organization;
  2. Articles of incorporation or business operation regulations equivalent thereto;
  3. Business plan;
  4. Status of possession of facilities and equipment.
  (3) If a person who has applied for designation in accordance with paragraph (2) satisfies the designation requirements in accordance with paragraph (1), the Administrator of the Korea Forest Service shall designate the person as a mountain village revitalization support center (hereinafter referred to as "support center") in accordance with Article 27-3 (1) of the Act, and issue a designation certificate as prescribed by the Ordinance of the Ministry of Agriculture, Food and Rural Affairs.
  (4) A person designated as a support center pursuant to paragraph (3) shall submit a business plan for the relevant year by January 31 of each year, and the business performance of the previous year by the end of the first quarter of each year, to the Administrator of the Korea Forest Service.
  (5) The "business prescribed by Presidential Decree" in Article 27-3 (2) 4 of the Act refers to the following business projects:
  1. Exchange support project between urban residents and mountain village residents who wish to settle in mountain villages;
  2. Support projects for local communities of mountain village residents;
  3. Creation of local forestry jobs and support and brokerage business for employment and start-up of mountain village residents;
  4. Exchange and cooperation projects with the support centers in other regions or the support institutions for the communities of mountain village residents.
  (6) The detailed criteria for the revocation of designation of a support center and corrective order pursuant to Article 27-3 (4) of the Act are as set forth in attached Table 2-2.
  [This Article Newly Inserted on Jun. 2, 2020]

Article 25 (Transfer of State or Public Forest Products) (1) The Administrator of the Korea Forest Service, the Mayor/Do Governor, or the head of a Si/Gun/Gu may, pursuant to Article 28 of the Act, provide the State or public forest products free of charge to the residents of the relevant mountain village development area, provided that they cooperate with the following matters: <Amended on Jun. 2, 2020>
  1. Prevention and suppression of forest fires;
  2. Prevention of illegal logging, excessive logging, unauthorized timber harvesting, or alteration of the topography of forest land;
  3. Prevention and control of forest pests and diseases;
  4. Mountain purification.
  (2) State or public forest products that the Administrator of the Korea Forest Service, a Mayor/Do Governor, or the head of a Si/Gun/Gu may gratuitously transfer to residents in mountain village development districts pursuant to Article 28 of the Act shall be as follows: <Amended on Jun. 2, 2020>
  1. Dead or fallen trees;
  2. Scrap wood and branches;
  3. Forest products harvested for the purpose of site preparation for afforestation and forest tending;
  4. Forest by-products that can be collected without changing the nature of the forest.
  (3) In order for the residents of mountain village development areas to receive free of charge the State or public forest products pursuant to paragraph (1), they shall submit an application (including an application in electronic form) as prescribed by the Ordinance of the Ministry of Agriculture, Food and Rural Affairs to the Administrator of the Korea Forest Service, the Mayor/Do Governor, or the head of a Si/Gun/Gu (including submission by electronic document). <Amended on Feb. 29, 2008; Mar. 23, 2013; Jun. 2, 2020>
  [Title Amended on Jun. 26, 2020]

CHAPTER VI KOREAN FORESTRY PROMOTION INSTITUTE

Article 25-2 (Establishment and Operation of the Korea Forestry Promotion Institute) (1) The Korea Forestry Promotion Institute pursuant to Article 29-2 (1) of the Act (hereinafter referred to as the "KOFPI") shall be established by registering its establishment at the location of its main office.
  (2) In order to carry out its duties, the KOFPI may, if necessary, establish subsidiary organizations such as branches, offices, and testing and analysis institutions at home and abroad in accordance with the provisions of the Articles of Incorporation.
  (3) The articles of incorporation of the KOFPI shall include the following matters:
  1. Objectives;
  2. Name;
  3. The seat of the principal office;
  4. Matters concerning the organization, including the chairperson, directors, executive agencies, and employees;
  5. Matters concerning the composition and operation of the board of directors;
  6. Matters concerning business affairs and the execution thereof;
  7. Matters concerning the establishment of subordinate organizations such as branches, business offices, and testing and analysis institutions;
  8. Matters concerning modifications to the articles of incorporation;
  9. Matters concerning the disposition of surplus and the amount of losses;
  10. Matters concerning property and accounting;
  11. Matters concerning the method of public announcement.
  [This Article Newly Inserted on Jan. 25, 2012]

Article 25-3 (Business of the Korea Forestry Promotion Institute) The term "business prescribed by Presidential Decree" in Article 29-3 (1) 9 of the Act refers to the following businesses: <Amended on Sep. 19, 2017; Jun. 2, 2020; Mar. 8, 2022; Mar. 14, 2023>
  1. Support for ecological experience of forest resources;
  2. Enhancement of brand value of forest resources and support therefor;
  3. International cooperation and human resource development required for forest resource management projects;
  4. Support for quality testing, inspection, manufacturing and import of forest biomass energy;
  5. Technology development, performance testing and distribution of combustors using forest biomass energy;
  6. Forest management certification and related consulting services;
  7. Certification of forest carbon sequestration and certification of carbon offset superior products;
  8. Forestry statistical surveys, including surveys on the economic conditions of forestry households, the status of forestry management, the production costs of forest products, the utilization of timber, and the operation and working conditions of forestry machinery and equipment;
  9. Production of forest topography maps and forest site maps, analysis and utilization of the results of the national forest resource survey;
  10. Establishment of a greenhouse gas statistics infrastructure, including the development of a forest aerial photography database;
  11. Support for forestry education at affiliated schools in the forestry and forest sector;
  12. Support for specialized mountain village development projects and income enhancement projects for mountain village residents;
  13. Support for settlement in mountain villages by foresters;
  14. Support for the production, processing, propagation, and distribution of forest products under special management and their seeds and seedlings;
  15. Support project for promoting export of forest products;
  16. Support projects for promotion of legal timber trade;
  17. Other projects necessary to achieve the purpose of establishment of the KOFPI.
  [This Article Newly Inserted on Jan. 25, 2012]

